GRUVAX uses Alembic with the async psycopg driver.

Each requested migration chain is one PostgreSQL transaction. A downgrade that
crosses revision 0010 or drops revision 0009 first locks the GRUVAX profile tables
and checks for non-default profile data, before any migration SQL runs. The same
guard is the first operation in both downgrade functions for direct/emitted SQL.
It refuses rather than deleting or deduplicating user metadata, PATs, collections,
settings, boundaries, overrides or statistics. A profile with no boundaries is
still protected. Transaction-owned locks prevent a concurrent profile writer
from racing the check and are released by commit or rollback.

Back up/export non-default profiles before planning a destructive rollback; the
migration never removes them automatically. Default-only upgrade/downgrade
round-trips remain supported. A refusal or later failure leaves the entire
requested chain's revision, schema, constraints and data unchanged.
